What is the TCM-CAS primary purpose?

The primary purpose of the TCM Constitution Assessment System (TCM-CAS) is to provide an end-to-end, automated solution for assessing a patient's health based on the principles of Traditional Chinese Medicine [1-6].

Specifically, the core goals of TCM-CAS are to:

Automatically Assess TCM Constitution (TCMC): The fundamental goal of the AI system is to automatically assess a patient's Traditional Chinese Medicine Constitution (TCMC) [1, 2, 4-17]. TCMC is a core concept in TCM theory that serves as the foundation for disease diagnosis and treatment [6, 13, 18-20].
Potentially Diagnose Diseases: The system is also designed to potentially diagnose diseases from a TCM perspective [2, 4-11, 13-17]. Furthermore, achieving TCMC assessment lays the groundwork for future capabilities in automatic disease diagnosis from a TCM perspective, provided sufficient annotated datasets are available [2, 6, 20-23].

Strategic and Technical Objectives
TCM-CAS was specifically proposed to address major challenges in traditional TCM assessment by integrating multiple technologies:

Objective Assessment: By integrating multimodal data (face images, tongue images, and questionnaire text), TCM-CAS aims to provide a more objective assessment to aid TCM researchers and practitioners [7, 23-29]. This helps standardize and enhance the precision and accessibility of TCM’s holistic health approach [24, 26, 27].
Bridging Traditional and Modern Wisdom: The system seeks to bridge traditional observational methods (like face reading) with data-driven precision from modern technology [7, 27, 30, 31].
Solving Technical Challenges: The system acts as an end-to-end solution for two main technical challenges:
Learning discriminative TCM clinical feature representations [2, 5, 18, 20, 22, 32].
Jointly processing these learned features using multimodal fusion techniques [2, 5, 18, 20, 22, 32].

Auxiliary Functions: TCM-CAS also includes auxiliary functions designed to aid TCM researchers, such as clinical feature analysis and data exploration [2, 5, 6, 20, 21, 33].

The system achieves its goals by combining multiple machine learning algorithms, such as facial landmark detection, image segmentation, graph neural networks, and multimodal fusion, to comprehensively process multimodal clinical information [2, 6, 8, 15, 20, 22, 24, 34, 35].

What are the Five Elements in TCM?

The Five Element Theory (Wu Xing or Five Phases) is a foundational concept in Traditional Chinese Medicine (TCM) philosophy [1-7]. Rooted in ancient Chinese cosmological notions, this comprehensive system classifies all phenomena into five interconnected elemental qualities [7-13].

The Five Elements themselves are [1, 7-10, 15-20]:
Wood, Fire, Earth, Metal, Water

Core Correspondences
Each element is associated with a specific Yin organ (Zang), a Yang organ (Fu), a corresponding flavor (or taste), color, and specific therapeutic actions [7-9, 14, 16-19, 21-24].

Element | Yin Organ (Zang) | Yang Organ (Fu) | Associated Flavor | Color
Wood | Liver | Gallbladder | Sour | Green
Fire | Heart | Small Intestine | Bitter | Red
Earth | Spleen | Stomach | Sweet | Yellow
Metal | Lung | Large Intestine | Pungent | White
Water | Kidney | Urinary Bladder | Salty | Black or Blue

Principles of Interaction and Dietary Therapy
The primary use of the Five Element Theory is in diagnosis and formulating treatment, particularly through dietary therapy [19, 40].

Interactions: The elements interact in two main cycles: the enhancing (or generating) cycle and the destructive (or overcoming/restraining) cycle [10, 12, 27].
Flavor Effects: The five flavors correspond to the five elements and are believed to have specific actions on particular organs (tropisms) [16, 31-33, 38].
Sweet flavors nourish the Spleen/Stomach.
Bitter flavors drain downward and dry fluids, associated with the Heart.
Sour flavors absorb and astringe, linked to the Liver/Gallbladder.
Salty flavors soften hard masses, linked to the Kidneys/Bladder.

Organ Support: To support an organ, TCM suggests consuming its corresponding flavor or the flavor corresponding to the element that precedes it in the generating cycle [16].
Seasonal Association: The year is divided into five seasons (Spring, Summer, Late Summer, Autumn, and Winter), each associated with a specific element [43, 44].

How many primary meridians exist?
Based on the information available in the sources, the number of primary meridians in Traditional Chinese Medicine (TCM) is consistently identified as 12.

Which organs are yin and yang or wood water fire wind have to do with healing?
The Traditional Chinese Medicine (TCM) system relies on the holistic framework of Yin and Yang and the Five Element Theory to classify internal organs, understand disease, and guide healing through specific flavors and energetic properties of food [1-4].

1. Organ Classification by Yin and Yang (Zàng-Fŭ)
The organs in TCM are categorized into two major groups based on their function and energetic nature (Yin or Yang).
Zàng (Solid Organs - Yin): Heart, Liver, Spleen, Lung, Kidney, and Pericardium. Function: Production and storage of Qi, Blood, Essence, Body Fluids.
Fǔ (Hollow Organs - Yang): Small Intestine, Large Intestine, Gallbladder, Urinary Bladder, Stomach, and San Jiao. Function: Transmit and digest substances.

2. Organ and Element Associations (The Five Elements)
Element | Zàng Organ (Yin) | Fǔ Organ (Yang) | Associated Flavor | Associated Climate
Wood | Liver | Gallbladder | Sour | Wind
Fire | Heart | Small Intestine | Bitter | Heat
Earth | Spleen | Stomach | Sweet | Dampness
Metal | Lung | Large Intestine | Pungent | Dryness
Water | Kidney | Bladder | Salty | Cold

3. Role in Healing and Dietary Therapy
The primary objective of TCM healing is to restore the balance between Yin and Yang and harmonize the Five Elements [24, 26-28]. This is achieved largely through dietary therapy, which emphasizes the healing effects of the five flavors on their corresponding organ systems [24, 29, 30].

Which two forces must balance?
The two fundamental forces that must be balanced, according to Traditional Chinese Medicine (TCM) and related philosophical concepts, are Yin and Yang [1-17].

